NAME
    readpass - read one secret line from the controlling terminal

SYNOPSIS
    readpass [<prompt>]

DESCRIPTION
    readpass reads one line from /dev/tty with terminal echo disabled.

    The command is a standalone POSIX sh utility and does not depend on the m
    bootstrap. Standard input is not consumed.

    An optional prompt is written directly to /dev/tty. Before reading,
    readpass saves the exact terminal state and disables echo. The saved state
    is restored before the secret is written to standard output.

    Leading and trailing IFS whitespace and backslashes in the entered line are
    preserved. As a line-oriented shell interface, the command does not
    represent embedded newline or NUL bytes.

OUTPUT
    On success, standard output contains exactly the captured secret followed
    by a newline.

    Prompt and terminal-interaction output are written to /dev/tty, not to
    standard output.

EXIT STATUS
    0   Secret read successfully.
    1   Terminal, input, restoration, output or handled-signal failure.
    2   Invalid invocation.

FILES
    /dev/tty
        Controlling terminal used for prompt, input and terminal-state changes.

SECURITY
    Standard output deliberately carries the secret. Callers should capture it
    into a shell variable or connect it only to the intended consumer rather
    than allowing it to reach an unintended terminal, log or file.

EXAMPLES
    password=$(readpass 'Password: ') || exit $?

SEE ALSO
    readpassv
    read-key
